    "text": "All evidence is in hand. Here is the analysis, grounded in the checkout at `/workspace` (paths below are repo-relative).\n\n## Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n- `init(optimize_threads=...)` (`python/zvec/zvec.py:29-43`) is mapped by the binding to `data.optimize_thread_count = 4` (`src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:154-158`).\n- `GlobalResource::initialize()` creates the global pool exactly once via `std::call_once`: `optimize_thread_pool_ = new ailego::ThreadPool(GlobalConfig::Instance().optimize_thread_count(), ...)` (`src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21-29`; accessor `src/db/common/global_resource.h:31-34`). The `ThreadPool` constructor clamps the requested size into `[1, SafeWorkerCount]` (`src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:93-101`), so under the stated assumptions the global optimize pool has **4 workers**.\n- `collection.optimize(option)` (`python/zvec/model/collection.py:231-238`) \u2192 binding `Optimize` (`src/binding/python/model/python_collection.cc:240-246`) \u2192 `CollectionImpl::optimize` (`src/db/collection.cc:952`), which passes `options.concurrency_` (`struct OptimizeOptions`, `src/include/zvec/db/options.h:60-62`) into the compact/merge tasks (`src/db/collection.cc:988-990`, `1128-1207`; `src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:640-744`).\n\n## 1. Pool selection per invocation\n\nThe selection point is identical in `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse` (`src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:804-811`) and `SegmentImpl::merge_vector_indexer` (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:1560-1567`):\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: `merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool()` and `write_concurrency = pool->count()` = 4 (`segment_helper.cc:805-808`).\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: only `write_concurrency = 2` is set; `pool` stays at its default `nullptr` (`segment_helper.cc:809-811`; default in `src/db/index/column/vector_column/vector_column_params.h:72-75`).\n\n`VectorColumnIndexer::merge` forwards `{write_concurrency, pool}` (`src/db/index/column/vector_column/vector_column_indexer.cc:128-130`) into core `Index::merge` (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1416-1446`), which finalizes the choice:\n\n- **A**: `options.pool != nullptr` \u2192 `reducer->set_thread_pool(options.pool)` \u2014 the **global 4-worker optimize pool**, borrowed; `effective_write_concurrency = min(4, pool->count())` (`index.cc:1436-1439`).\n- **B**: a **fresh, merge-local `ailego::ThreadPool(2, false)`** is created and handed to the reducer (`index.cc:1440-1445`); the comment at `index.cc:1433` notes it is declared so its lifetime covers `reducer->reduce()`, and it is destroyed when `Index::merge` returns. `set_thread_pool` stores a non-owning raw pointer (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_reducer.h:173-175, 183`).\n\n**Does B change the global pool? No.** The `GlobalResource` pool is touched only in the `concurrency == 0` branch; it is created once under `std::call_once` (`global_resource.cc:22-23`) and nothing in the `concurrency != 0` path resizes or reconfigures it. After B, the global pool remains the 4-worker pool configured by `init`.\n\n## 2. Handoff to the target builder\n\nA single handoff point in `MixedStreamerReducer::index_build` (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:742-777`):\n\n- One `BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads` is constructed over `*thread_pool_` (line 743-744). This adapter is non-owning (reference member, `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174-225`, esp. 224), reports `count()` from the underlying pool (184-186), and `submit()` enqueues onto that same pool (194-199).\n- **Optional conversion training**: `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(target_builder_converter_, target_holder, threads)` (lines 745-747), which forwards the threads into `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))` (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:27-36`, esp. 31).\n- **Then the target builder**: `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)` (line 760) and `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)` (line 768) \u2014 the same wrapper over the same pool (base virtual `train(IndexThreads::Pointer, ...)`: `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_trainer.h:92`).\n\nSo in A the conversion-training call and the builder's train/build all run against the global 4-worker pool; in B, against the ephemeral 2-worker pool. The important distinction the question flags: **passing the pool does not prove consumption**. Some implementations ignore the argument entirely \u2014 the default runner overloads comment the parameter out (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_runner.h:692-760`), and e.g. `FlatSparseBuilder::train` takes an unnamed `IndexThreads::Pointer` (`src/core/algorithm/flat_sparse/flat_sparse_builder.cc:60-61`). `DiskAnnBuilder` is a consumer (next section).\n\n## 3. DiskANN PQ training: the outer chunk-dispatch pool is *not* reused\n\n- `DiskAnnBuilder::train(threads, holder)` (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:633-681`) receives the non-null borrowed threads (so its private fallback at 657-663 is skipped) and calls `train_quantized_data(threads)` (665).\n- `train_quantized_data` (378-427) does **not** hand its `threads` to the quantizer. It computes a size instead \u2014 with the explicit comment *\"The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool\"*:\n  `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` (lines 391-394), passed as the quantizer's `\"thread_count\"` param. `build_thread_count_` is the builder's own setting (`PARAM_DISKANN_BUILDER_THREAD_COUNT`, line 51), defaulting to `hardware_concurrency()` when 0 (53-55).\n- `PqInt8Quantizer::init` stores it in `thread_count_` (`src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142`; member default 0, `pq_int8_quantizer.h:217`), and `train` (line 232) reaches `train_all_chunks` (305), which **creates a brand-new owned pool**: `SingleQueueIndexThreads(thread_count_, false)` (322-323; owns an `ailego::ThreadPool` by value, `index_threads.h:162-167`) and dispatches PQ chunks across its workers with a stride loop (328-343).\n\nSo: the PQ-training outer pool is a **transient, private pool**, not the caller's pool. Its worker count is `max(1, min(builder's build_thread_count_, caller pool's count))` \u2014 under the scenario's assumptions (builder param unset \u2192 `build_thread_count_ = hardware_concurrency \u2265 4`): **4 workers in A** (min(hw, 4)) and **2 workers in B** (min(hw, 2)); an explicit builder setting *k* would give min(k, 4) / min(k, 2). This contrasts with DiskANN's other stages, which *do* reuse the borrowed pool directly: `build_internal` and `prune_internal` submit `threads->count()` tasks via `threads->make_group()` (`diskann_builder.cc:304-315`, `341-352`), and `generate_quantized_data` stripes encoding across the caller's threads (451-479).\n\n## 4. What the settings establish \u2014 and what they don't\n\n**Established (capacity facts):**\n- The global optimize pool has exactly `optimize_thread_count` = 4 workers, created once (`global_resource.cc:21-29`), subject to the `ThreadPool` clamp to `[1, SafeWorkerCount]` (`thread_pool.cc:93-101`).\n- Invocation A's builder-side work (conversion train, builder train/build, reducer add stage at `mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:211-236`) executes on that 4-worker pool; B's executes on a purpose-built 2-worker pool that dies with the merge (`index.cc:1433-1445`), leaving the global pool unmodified.\n- DiskANN PQ training runs on a private pool whose size is the min-clamped value above (`diskann_builder.cc:391-394`, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:319-328`).\n\n**Not established:**\n- **Whole-process thread count.** These knobs bound only the pools they configure. Other threads coexist independently: the query pool (`global_resource.cc:24-26`), per-merge local pools, quantizer-private pools, DiskANN's fallback pools when `threads == nullptr` (`diskann_builder.cc:657-663`, `714-720`), plus Python/OS and DiskANN I/O-backend threads. `optimize_threads=4` is not a process-wide cap.\n- **Actual CPU utilization.** Pool size is capacity, not occupancy. Workers can sit idle; several stages are serial (e.g., medoid calculation and `calculate_pq_chunk_num`, `diskann_builder.cc:168-302`); task granularity can under-fill a pool (PQ training submits `pool_count` tasks that stride over `num_chunk_` chunks \u2014 if chunks < workers, extra workers get no work, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:330-343`); and requested sizes can be clamped (`thread_pool.cc:93-101`). Whether 4 workers are busier than 2 depends on data volume, memory limits, and stage mix, none of which these settings determine."
